{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during a critical era of the Crusades. He held this title from 1152 until his death in 1187. His life became deeply intertwined with the political struggles of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond served as regent for the young King Baldwin V and acted as a key advisor to Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. The relationship between these two men was complex yet vital for the stability of the realm.\n\n### Early Life and Inheritance\nRaymond was born into a prominent family with strong ties to the Latin East. His father Raymond II had been assassinated by the Hashashin sect in 1152. This tragic event thrust the young Raymond into power while he was still a child. He inherited vast lands and responsibilities that required immediate attention from his mentors. The Assassins continued to threaten the region even after his father death. Raymond spent his early years consolidating his authority against various internal rivals. He eventually secured his position as one of the most influential barons in the kingdom. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures further expanded his holdings and influence within the region.\n\n### Regency and Political Struggles\nThe death of King Amalric in 1174 left Baldwin IV as a minor king suffering from a debilitating disease. Raymond stepped forward to serve as regent during this turbulent time. He managed the affairs of state with skill and caution. His tenure saw several attempts to manage the rising threat of Saladin. Raymond often advocated for diplomacy over rash military engagements. He believed that direct confrontation with Saladin could lead to disaster for the fragile Crusader states. This approach sometimes put him at odds with other nobles who favored aggressive action. The faction led by Guy of Lusignan opposed Raymond constantly. Their rivalry threatened to tear the kingdom apart from within.\n\n### The Battle of Hattin and Death\nThe final years of Raymond life were marked by increasing tension and military failure. He fell out with Guy of Lusignan who became king through marriage. Raymond refused to join Guy's army against Saladin in 1187. This decision allowed Saladin to defeat the Crusader forces decisively at the Battle of Hattin. Raymond managed to escape the massacre but his reputation suffered greatly. He died shortly after the battle in 1187. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. The loss of such a capable leader weakened the defenses of the Latin East significantly. Historical accounts often debate his true motives during these final conflicts. Some view him as a pragmatic survivor while others see him as a traitor to the cause. His legacy remains a subject of intense historical analysis and debate among scholars today.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0005801214}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0039718845}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.13543326}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0072614104}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.91292673}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.00962957}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.17278364}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.021801004}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.0814072}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.037362248}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00048673074}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0047590546}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -3.2536895}, {"token": " critical", "logprob": -1.8649527}, {"token": " era", "logprob": -0.80456024}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.22067983}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.19491096}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.006019434}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.019499844}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.008723228}, {"token": " He", "logprob